353:
(a) The clerk shall issue a written notice of intent to revoke a sexually oriented business license or a sexually oriented business employee license, as applicable, if the licensee knowingly violates this article or has knowingly allowed an employee to violate this article and a suspension of the licensee's license has become effective within the previous 12-month period.
(b) The clerk shall issue a written notice of intent to revoke a sexually oriented business license or a sexually oriented business employee license, as applicable, if:
(1) The licensee has knowingly given false information in the application for the sexually oriented business license or the sexually oriented business employee license;
(2) The licensee has knowingly or recklessly engaged in or allowed possession, use, or sale of controlled substances on the premises of the sexually oriented business;
(3) The licensee has knowingly or recklessly engaged in or allowed prostitution on the premises of the sexually oriented business;
(4) The licensee knowingly or recklessly operated the sexually oriented business during a period of time when the license was finally suspended or revoked; or
(5) The licensee has knowingly or recklessly engaged in or allowed any specified sexual activity to occur in or on the premises of the sexually oriented business.
(c) The fact that any relevant conviction is being appealed shall have no effect on the revocation of the license, provided that, if any conviction which serves as a basis of a license revocation is overturned or reversed on appeal, that conviction shall be treated as null and of no effect for revocation purposes.
(d) When, after the notice and hearing procedure described in this article, the county revokes a license, the revocation shall continue for one year and the licensee shall not be issued a sexually oriented business license or sexually oriented business employee license for one year from the date revocation becomes effective.
(Ord. of 4-7-2009(2), § 10)

392:
(a) Daily report. Every licensee shall maintain a permanent electronic record of all property pledged to, traded, bought, or sold by licensee during the 24 hours ending at 8:00 p.m. on the date of the report so that an accurate description can be transmitted to the county police department and the county sheriff's department via an electronic automated reporting system. Each electronic record shall include at a minimum:
(1) The name and address of the licensee;
(2) The date and time of the pawn, trade, purchase, or sale of property;
(3) Serial number of pawn tickets;
(4) Amount paid or advanced;
(5) Full description of articles, including kind, style, material, color, design, kind and number of stones in jewelry, and all identifying names, marks and serial numbers;
(6) A description of persons pledging, selling, purchasing, or pawning, including name, address, phone number, driver's license number or government issued identification card number, date of birth, sex, weight, and height;
(7) A photograph of the customer and the item pawned, traded, bought, or sold which will be taken with the electronic automated reporting system at the time of the transaction; and
(8) The signature of the customer.
Serial numbers on items with same must be included in this report. Insufficient reports shall be rejected, and any licensee, or employee thereof, making same shall be deemed guilty of an offense. Any person subject to this article, any licensee or employee thereof, who shall make false entries concerning the transactions named shall be guilty of an offense.
(b) Every licensee shall enter each transaction as it occurs into the electronic automated reporting system or may elect to upload electronically via the internet in a batch of all transactions for each business day to the administrator of the electronic reporting system immediately at the conclusion of each business day. The administrator of the electronic automated reporting system will electronically transmit all transactions to the county police department and sheriff's department.
(c) In the event that the electronic automated reporting system becomes temporarily or permanently disabled, licensees will be notified as soon as possible. In this event, the licensee will be required to make records of transactions in paper form. Such paper forms must include all information as enumerated in subsection (a). The licensee shall maintain a three-day supply of these paper forms.
(d) Any duly authorized law enforcement officer may, during the ordinary hours of business or at any other reasonable time, inspect any licensee's electronic records and inventory at the licensee's place of business to ensure compliance with this section.
(e) The chief of police or his designee shall select and designate the required automated reporting system. There will be a fee assessed to the licensee for each reported transaction; said fee shall be set by the system vendor. This fee will be invoiced to the licensee and collected by the system vendor.
(f) Only transaction records containing complete information shall be deemed to comply with this section.
(g) Violation. The failure of any licensee or employee thereof to comply with the provisions of this section shall constitute an offense.
(Ord. of 7-14-2015)

403:
The following words, terms or phrases, when used in this article, shall have the meaning ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
Amusement game room means location as provided in O.C.G.A. § 16-12-35(b), (c) or (d) where one or more bona fide coin-operated amusement machines are operated that permit non-cash redemption as provided in O.C.G.A. § 16-12-35(d)(1)(B), (C), or a combination thereof.
Bona fide coin-operated amusement machine means the same as this term is defined in O.C.G.A. § 48-17-1(2)(A) and (B) and any applicable regulations of the State of Georgia. Examples of bona fide coin-operated amusement machines include, but are expressly not limited to, the following:
(1) Pinball machines;
(2) Console machines;
(3) Video games;
(4) Crane machines;
(5) Claw machines;
(6) Pusher machines;
(7) Bowling machines;
(8) Novelty arcade games;
(9) Foosball or table soccer machines;
(10) Miniature racetrack, football or golf machines;
(11) Target or shooting gallery machines;
(12) Basketball machines;
(13) Shuffleboard machines;
(14) Kiddie ride games;
(15) Skeeball machines;
(16) Air hockey machines;
(17) Roll down machines;
(18) Coin-operated pool table or coin-operated billiard table as defined in paragraph (3) of O.C.G.A. § 43-8-1; and
(19) Any other similar amusement machine which can be legally operated in Georgia.
The term "coin-operated amusement machine" does not include the following:
(20) Coin-operated washing machines or dryers;
(21) Vending machines which for payment of money dispense products or services;
(22) Gas and electric meters;
(23) Pay telephones;
(24) Pay toilets;
(25) Cigarette vending machines;
(26) Coin-operated vending machines;
(27) Coin-operated scales;
(28) Coin-operated gumball machines;
(29) Coin-operated television sets which provide cable or network programming;
(30) Coin-operated massage beds; and
(31) Machines which are not legally permitted to be operated in Georgia.
Locations means a business within the county that has complied with the provisions of the ordinances of the county relating to occupation taxes and business licenses.
(Ord. of 5-4-2010, § 1)

405:
Application of a license for operating an amusement game room within the county shall be made to the commission upon a form to be supplied by the county clerk for this purpose. The license application shall include the following information:
(1) Name, address and age of the applicant and the date of the application;
(2) Address or place where the bona fide coin-operated amusement machine or machines are to be offered to the public for play and the other business or businesses operated at that place or places;
(3) Name and address of the owner of the machine or machines and a copy of the owner's master license;
(4) Name and address of any other business owned or operated by applicant within the county;
(5) List of any other licenses or permits from the county held by the applicant.
Upon issuing a license for an amusement game room, the county official or employee shall provide the licensee with a copy of the ordinance codified in this article. The county shall not require a fee for licensure or registration of an amusement game room. A license issued in accordance with this article shall be valid until December 31 of the year in which the license was issued. The owner or operator of an amusement game room shall be required to pay any occupation taxes imposed by the county in accordance with this Code. See fees as referenced in Subpart A, Chapter 1, Section 1-17 - Fee Schedule.
(Ord. of 5-4-2010, § 1; Ord. No. 2019-04, § 1, 2-5-2019)

468:
The board of commissioners of the county finds that there exist in the county, dwellings, buildings, or structures, or recreational vehicles which:
(1) Are unfit for human habitation or for commercial, industrial, or business uses due to dilapidation and not in compliance with applicable codes;
(2) Have defects increasing the hazards of fire, accidents, or other calamities;
(3) Lack adequate ventilation, light or sanitary facilities;
(4) Other conditions exist rendering such dwellings, buildings, or structures, or recreational vehicles unsafe or unsanitary, or dangerous or detrimental to the health, safety, or welfare, or otherwise inimical to the welfare of the residents of the county or vacant, dilapidated dwellings, buildings, or structures, or recreational vehicles in which drug crimes are being committed; or
(5) Recreational vehicles are being occupied as living quarters or has a permanent residential utility connection (ex. Hard piped/wired, etc.)
(Ord. of 11-9-2004, art. III, § 2; Ord. No. 2022-55, § 1, 3-1-2022; Res. No. 2024-107, § 1, 2-6-2024; Res. No. 2025-164, § 1, 9-2-2025)

479:
(a) The public officer may determine, under existing ordinances, that a building, dwelling, or structure, or recreational vehicle is unfit for human habitation or is unfit for its current commercial, industrial or business use if he or she finds that conditions exist in such building, dwelling, or structure, or recreational vehicle which are dangerous or injurious to the health, safety or morals of the occupants of such building, dwelling, or structure, or recreational vehicle; or the occupants of such neighborhood buildings, dwellings, or structures; or of other residents of the county. Such conditions may include the following, without limiting the generality of the foregoing:
(1) Defects therein increasing the hazards of fire, accidents or other calamities;
(2) Lack of adequate ventilation, light or sanitary facilities;
(3) Dilapidation;
(4) Disrepair;
(5) Structural defects; or
(6) Uncleanliness.
(b) The public officer may determine under existing ordinances that a building, dwelling or structure, or recreational vehicle is vacant, dilapidated and being used in connection with the commission of drug crimes upon personal observation or report of a law enforcement agency and evidence of drug crimes being committed.
(Ord. of 11-9-2004, art. IV, pt. 1, § 8; Ord. No. 2022-55, § 1, 3-1-2022)
